<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>en</string>
    <!-- What the home screen shows under the icon: NEON.

         The app was S5LBox, named for the S5L8900 chip it emulates, until
         2026-09-25, when the owner renamed it NEON as the project turned
         towards ARMv7 and iOS 6 on the iPhone 3GS. (Not to be confused with
         ARM's NEON, the Advanced SIMD instructions the Cortex-A8 profile
         executes; the docs call that "Advanced SIMD (NEON)".)

         CFBundleName follows PRODUCT_NAME, which is NEON as well, as are the
         Xcode target, the .app, the .ipa and the entitlements file. The
         bundle identifier is still com.j0shua.S5LBox on purpose: it is never
         shown, and keeping it makes a NEON install update an S5LBox already
         on the phone in place, with its firmware, machines and settings.
         Internal names that nobody sees (build settings, file-format markers,
         accessibility identifiers) keep the old spelling for the same
         reason. -->
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>$(S5LBOX_DISPLAY_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.0.1</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>2</string>
    <key>S5LBoxSourceRevision</key>
    <string>$(S5LBOX_SOURCE_REVISION)</string>
    <!-- The app's Documents folder appears in Files under "On My iPhone",
         which is how a 239 MB IPSW gets in without going through a document
         picker: copy it into NEON's folder and the Firmware screen finds it
         where it already is.

         WHAT THIS EXPOSES, stated because it is not only the firmware. The
         same folder holds Machines/<id>/rootfs-work.img – a whole per-machine
         work image, around 445 MB each – and those become visible, movable
         and deletable. That is deliberate: a work image the user cannot copy
         off the device is a VM they cannot back up, and every emulator that
         ships on iOS makes the same trade.

         It also means the canonical firmware inputs can be overwritten by the
         user. That is already handled rather than newly risky: VMFirmwareBoot
         gates every one of the three on size AND SHA-256 before it is opened,
         so a replaced or truncated input is refused by name instead of being
         booted. The gate existed before this key did.

         LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace lets the importer read an archive
         where it sits rather than copying it into the container first, which
         on a 64 GB phone is the difference between needing 239 MB free and
         needing 478 MB. -->
    <key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
    <true/>
    <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
    <true/>
    <key>MinimumOSVersion</key>
    <string>13.0</string>
    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict/>
    <key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
    <array>
        <string>arm64</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
    </array>
    <key>UIStatusBarStyle</key>
    <string>UIStatusBarStyleLightContent</string>
</dict>
</plist>
